+# Entropica — Commercial Readiness Checklist
+
+Quick, practical list to move from research prototype → sellable product.
+Items are ordered roughly by impact / dependency. Mark progress as you go.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Core technical credibility (must-have before any sales conversation)
+
+- [x] **Structural signal for templated IDs** (prefix + varying-suffix entropy)  
+  Closed the exact blind spot real VAmPI data exposed. Metrics + explain layer + tests shipped.
+- [ ] **Clean real-traffic methodology**  
+  Separate organic target data from any script-injected samples. Label contaminated records clearly or drop them. Credibility is zero if a reviewer spots 93 % self-generated usernames.
+- [ ] **Demonstrate catching a known vulnerability end-to-end**  
+  VAmPI BOLA (username swap) and/or crAPI SSRF. Not just “calibration looks good” — show the tool would have produced a Finding on the real vulnerable behaviour.
+- [ ] **Threshold policy document**  
+  Synthetic + real-data rationale for every default (entropy 3.0, keyspace 32, sequential 0.7, templated heuristics). “Advisory only” notes stay visible; customers need to understand what they are tuning.
+- [ ] **Regression suite that includes the real-data patterns**  
+  bookTitle-style, short numeric tokens, auto-increment, UUIDv4, base62, contaminated vs clean samples. Keep the synthetic generators; add the real fixtures.
+
+## 2. Product surface (what a paying user actually touches)
+
+- [ ] **Persistent storage** (SQLite is already planned and the store is isolated)  
+  Findings survive process restarts; basic query by target / rule / severity / time.
+- [ ] **Auth on the control plane**  
+  At minimum API-key or basic auth. Do not leave an unauthenticated FastAPI surface on a customer network.
+- [ ] **Stable CLI + versioned HTTP API**  
+  Clear contracts, deprecation policy, semantic versioning. Current demo.py is fine for research; commercial needs a real entry point.
+- [ ] **Human + machine readable reports**  
+  Lean hard on the existing `explain.py` layer. PDF / Markdown / SARIF (or equivalent) export. “Bits and z-scores → language a product owner understands” is a selling point.
+- [ ] **Safe-by-default probing**  
+  Document and enforce the self-governing queue model (μ adaptation, backoff). Explicit rate limits, scope files, dry-run mode, and a clear “you must have permission” statement.
+
+## 3. Packaging, ops & legal hygiene
+
+- [ ] **Proper packaging**  
+  setup.py / pyproject.toml with pinned deps, optional extras, entry points. Version already at 0.2.0 — decide the next public number.
+- [ ] **Documentation that a buyer can evaluate in 15 minutes**  
+  What it detects, what it does *not* detect, how the math works at a high level, how to run a first scan, how to interpret a Finding.
+- [ ] **LICENSE + copyright**  
+  Currently MIT with placeholder “[Your Name]”. Decide final copyright holder and whether commercial use needs a dual-license or commercial addendum.
+- [ ] **Security & privacy posture**  
+  No accidental logging of secrets/tokens, clear data-retention story, no phoning home by default.
+- [ ] **Dependency & supply-chain baseline**  
+  Known vulnerabilities in httpx/fastapi/etc. tracked; lockfile or pinned ranges.
+
+## 4. Commercial model & go-to-market (decide early)
+
+- [ ] **Positioning**  
+  Specialist (best-in-class predictable-ID / BOLA + structural signals) vs broader OWASP API coverage?  
+  Open-core / pure OSS + paid support / fully commercial self-hosted / SaaS?
+- [ ] **Target buyer**  
+  AppSec engineer, product security team, pentester, or platform team embedding the engine?
+- [ ] **Differentiation statement (one paragraph)**  
+  “Mathematical, explainable signals instead of black-box rules or opaque ML. Real traffic revealed gaps synthetic data cannot — and we close them with first-principles math.”
+- [ ] **Pricing & packaging sketch**  
+  Even a rough “community free / pro paid features” outline helps decide which checklist items are free vs paid.
+- [ ] **Support & SLA story**  
+  What happens when a customer opens a ticket about a false positive or a missed templated pattern?
+
+## 5. Validation before first paid pilot
+
+- [ ] At least one external reviewer (friendly AppSec person or researcher) has run it against a known vulnerable app and confirmed Findings make sense.
+- [ ] False-positive rate on a small “known-good” set of modern UUID/token APIs is documented and acceptable.
+- [ ] Capture scripts and calibration harness are reproducible by someone other than you.
+- [ ] README + checklist above are honest about current limitations (no distributed workers yet, SQLite still planned, real-traffic pass still required for final threshold lock-in, etc.).
+
+---
+
+### Suggested next 2-week focus
+1. Finish capture hygiene + real known-vuln demonstration (items in section 1).  
+2. SQLite persistence + basic auth (section 2).  
+3. Write the short differentiation paragraph and decide open-core vs commercial license (section 4).  
+
+Everything else becomes much easier once those three are solid.
+
+*Last updated: 2026-08-31 — after shipping the templated-ID / prefix+suffix structural signal.*
